cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "dual-nonlinear-glu: revisiting the linear value branch of gated layers"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
version: 0.2.0
date-released: 2026-09-20
authors:
  - alias: PureTearsDropped
repository-code: "https://github.com/PureTearsDropped/dual-nonlinear-glu"
license: 0BSD
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22851805
abstract: >-
  Gated layers in transformers keep their value branch linear: SwiGLU is
  silu(W_g h) * (W_u h). Making both branches nonlinear is not a new design --
  it is the Gated Tanh Unit, which Dauphin et al. (2017) measured and rejected
  because both branches contribute a gradient downscaling factor, and the
  GLU -> GEGLU -> SwiGLU line descends from that rejection. On a synthetic
  teacher-student benchmark with a residual and a 1/sqrt(depth) branch
  initialisation, that rejection does not reproduce.

  The larger finding is about a different property. The proposed pair f and g,
  like max(ax,-b) and the identity, are all linear ABOVE zero, and it is that
  linear positive half which costs. Removing it from the gate -- a plain
  b*tanh(a x/b) on both sides -- is worth 1.5-5.9x in all four cells of two
  teachers by two value branches; removing it from the value branch is
  1.0-3.0x with one cell where it does nothing. The two changes are redundant
  rather than additive. This is the one effect that two teachers giving
  otherwise opposite accounts both support.

  A control separates the mechanism. A fixed tanh, with nothing learned, keeps
  most of the gain when the value branch is nonlinear but falls below SwiGLU
  when it is not, because it sits in its own linear region (median argument
  0.378) while a learned gate drives itself into saturation (median 3.2). What
  the learned scale buys is the operating point, and that decides the outcome
  only when the gate is the sole nonlinearity. The saturated gate is not a
  sign function -- that was tested and is wrong, at 3.70x -- but a clamp costs
  only 1.19x, and training with the clamp from the start costs 9%, so the gate
  needs no transcendental at all.

  Also recorded: asinh in the value branch can be evaluated by polynomials
  alone, with segment boundaries taken from the measured argument distribution
  rather than from the shape of asinh, reproducing a trained network's loss to
  seven figures and costing 30x on a GPU, where it must not be used; the
  block's output is the mixed second derivative of an elementary potential
  F(u)G(v), which survives the both-sides form; initialising the residual
  branch at 1/sqrt(depth) outweighs every activation choice measured;
  curvature-aware ternary rounding was derived, corrected, and failed; and a
  benchmark checklist for detecting tasks that do not need depth. Several
  claims published in v0.1.0 are corrected here, including two measured
  statements about GPU kernel cost and the attribution of a 2.1x gain to the
  wrong one of two simultaneous changes.
keywords:
  - activation function
  - gated linear unit
  - SwiGLU
  - gated tanh unit
  - residual network
  - benchmark methodology
  - negative results
  - polynomial approximation
  - Remez algorithm
  - hardware inference
